dhewm3 for RISC OS, version 0.2.2
=================================

dhewm3 is the Doom 3 engine (id Software's GPL source, kept up by the dhewm3
team). This is its RISC OS port. It draws with the GPU through RISC3D, and
needs RISC OS 5 on ARMv7 or later. It plays the free Doom 3 demo, and the full
game if you have its data (see "The full game" below).

Home page: https://davidsharp.com/riscos/doom3/
Contact (questions, bug reports): https://davidsharp.com/contact/

It runs on:
- a Raspberry Pi 4 running RISC OS 5.30, drawing on its GPU through RISC3D
  0.22 (V3DRender 0.37). The demo and the full game play at about 10 to 15
  frames a second in an 800 x 600 window. Tested on a 4 GB Pi 4;
- RISC OS on Linux, on a machine whose GPU Linux drives (not yet tested).

You need !RISC3D (0.22 or later on a Pi 4) in !Boot.Resources, and
SharedUnixLibrary (1.16 or later) and ARMEABISupport (1.03 or later). PackMan
installs all three for you.


Playing
-------

Double-click !dhewm3: its icon appears on the icon bar. Click the icon to
play. The game runs in an 800 x 600 desktop window. Alt+Return switches to
full screen and back; full screen keeps the desktop's own screen mode, so on a
Pi 4 it is slower (about 6 frames a second at 1280 x 1024). Use a 16 million
colour desktop.

The first time, there is no game data, so clicking the icon offers to
download the free Doom 3 demo:
- It downloads id Software's own Linux demo installer
  (doom3-linux-1.1.1286-demo.x86.run, 485 MB) from a mirror of id's FTP
  site, and checks it (SHA-256).
- id's licence for the demo then opens in your text editor. The demo is
  installed only if you accept it; if you decline, the download is
  deleted.
- The demo's data file goes into !dhewm3.demo (demo00/pk4, 484 MB) and
  the installer is deleted. Then the game starts.
Allow about 1 GB of free space while that happens.

If you already have the installer, drag it onto the icon instead.

id's licence doesn't allow the demo to be passed on, so it isn't in this
package: it comes from id's archive when you ask for it.

To see the frame rate: open the console (Shift+Esc), type  com_showFPS 1
and press Return.

To play faster, turn off the shadows: in the console, type  r_shadows 0
(about a third faster on a Pi 4: 13 to 18 frames a second in the hangar).
To keep it, add the line  set r_shadows 0  to
!dhewm3.home.dhewm3.base.autoexec/cfg, outside the part !dhewm3 marks.
r_skipSpecular 1 (no shiny highlights) gains a little more, about 1 frame
a second.


The full game
-------------

Buy Doom 3 from Steam or GOG: the original "DOOM 3", not "DOOM 3: BFG
Edition", whose data dhewm3 can't read. Install it on a PC or Mac, then copy
its base folder into !dhewm3, so that you have !dhewm3.base. On Steam the
folder is steamapps/common/Doom 3/base.

Copy only pak000 to pak008 (the .pk4 files) from base: they are about 1.6 GB.
Leave out the other folders (d3xp, Docs and so on). Keep them as pk4 files
(type FFF is fine). With the base folder there, a click on the icon plays the
full game; without it, the free demo.

On a Raspberry Pi 4 the full game needs more of the GPU's memory than RISC3D
gives programs by default (256 MB), and about 700 MB of free memory, so:
- Before the full game starts, !dhewm3 gives the GPU a 1 GB area for its
  buffers. It does this itself: it restarts RISC3D's GPU driver (V3DRender)
  with the bigger area, which lasts until you restart the computer. It can only
  do that while no other program is using the GPU; if one is, it asks you to
  quit it first.
- It plays the full game with smaller textures (at most 256 x 256), as
  Doom 3's own lower quality settings do. With full-size textures the game
  needs nearly all of the 1 GB, and can then stop with an error. The settings
  are in a marked part of !dhewm3.home.dhewm3.base.autoexec/cfg, which
  !dhewm3 rewrites at each start; the rest of that file is yours.
  To try full-size textures anyway (it needs about 960 MB free):
      *Set dhewm3$Textures full
- A Raspberry Pi 4 with 1 GB of memory can't run the full game; the demo is
  fine.

Loading a level of the full game takes about a minute and a half on a Pi 4.


Known problems
--------------

- On a Pi 4, a few GPU resets can happen while a level loads in full screen;
  the game carries on.
- A 64K colour desktop shows the game's window garbled: use a 16 million
  colour mode.


Files
-----

!dhewm3.home      settings and saved games
!dhewm3.stdout    the game's log (and stderr: its errors)
!dhewm3.launcherlog  the launcher's log (downloads, installs, GPU set-up)


